{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_lrkjv4gha7cgfumu3rsdsdhxfhb2qa2xz7becrjwndfzkysmcwzq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Let me create a unit test inline. Actually the simplest approach: write a test that computes blake256 of empty bytes and compares to the known value 0x716f6e863f744b9ac22c97ec7b76ea5f5908bc5b2f67c61510bfc4751384ea7a... \n\nLet me check the standard BLAKE-256 test vectors. According to Wikipedia, BLAKE-256(\"\") = 0x716f6e863f744b9ac22c97ec7b76ea5f5908bc5b2f67c61510bfc4751384ea7a\n\nLooking again at the C code more carefully, I notice the rotation:\n\n```c\n#define BLAKE256_ROT(x,n) (((x)<<(32-n))|( (x)>>(n)))\n```\n\nThis is `(x << (32-n)) | (x >> n)` — this is a rotate-LEFT by (32-n), or equivalently rotate-RIGHT by n. So `rotate_right(n)` is correct.\n\nBut wait, I need to double-check. Let me trace:\n- For BLAKE-256, the rotations are 16, 12, 8, 7. \n- The reference G function in BLAKE has: a += b + m_x; d ^= a; d = rot(d, 16); c += d; b ^= c; b = rot(b, 12); ...\n- In the standard BLAKE spec, those rotations are RIGHT rotations.\n- The C macro `((x<<(32-n))|(x>>n))` for n=16 is `((x<<16)|(x>>16))` which is rotate RIGHT by 16. ✓\n\nOK so my understanding is correct. Let me look at the C macro more carefully:\n\n```c\n#define ROUND(m0,c0,m1,c1,...,m15,c15) \\\n    v0 += m0 ^ c0; \\\n    v0 += v4; \\\n    v12 ^= v0; \\\n    v12 = BLAKE256_ROT( v12,16); \\\n    v8 += v12; \\\n    v4 ^= v8; \\\n    v4 = BLAKE256_ROT( v4,12); \\\n```\n\nSo G(a=0, b=4, c=8, d=12) takes the args `m0,c0,m1,c1`, but the **first** half of G uses `m0 ^ c0` and the **second** half uses `m1 ^ c1`. Looking at the call:\n\n```\nROUND(m0,cst[1],m1,cst[0],m2,cst[3],m3,cst[2],...)\n```\n\nThe first round (round 0 of sigma) gets `m0,c1,m1,c0,...`. So G(0,4,8,12) uses `m0^c1` first, `m1^c0` second.\n\nNow in my Rust code:\n```\ng!(v, 0, 4, 8, 12, m[sg[0]], CST_256[sg[1]], m[sg[1]], CST_256[sg[0]]);\n```\nWith `sg = [0, 1, 2, 3, ...]` for round 0:\n- `m[0] ^ CST_256[1]` for first half ✓\n- `m[1] ^ CST_256[0]` for second half ✓\n\nThe G macro is:\n```rust\n$v[$a] += $v[$b] + ($mx ^ $cx);  // first half mx^cx\n$v[$d] = rot32($v[$d] ^ $v[$a], 16);\n$v[$c] += $v[$d];\n$v[$b] = rot32($v[$b] ^ $v[$c], 12);\n$v[$a] += $v[$b] + ($my ^ $cy);  // second half my^cy\n$v[$d] = rot32($v[$d] ^ $v[$a], 8);\n$v[$c] += $v[$d];\n$v[$b] = rot32($v[$b] ^ $v[$c], 7);\n```\n\nThat looks right. The issue may be subtle. Let me look at the G call sequence in the round macro more carefully.\n\nLooking at the C ROUND macro - it interleaves the 8 G calls. The order is:\n- G(0,4,8,12) - cols 1+2 (first half: m0,c0; second half: m1,c1)\n- G(1,5,9,13) - cols (m2,c2 / m3,c3)\n- G(2,6,10,14) - (m4,c4 / m5,c5)\n- G(3,7,11,15) - (m6,c6 / m7,c7)\n- G(0,5,10,15) - diags (m8,c8 / m9,c9)\n- G(1,6,11,12) - (m10,c10 / m11,c11)\n- G(2,7,8,13) - (m12,c12 / m13,c13)\n- G(3,4,9,14) - (m14,c14 / m15,c15)\n\nThat matches my Rust code. Hmm.\n\nWait, let me re-look at the unsigned subtraction - in `s.t[0] -= 440 - s.buflen`. If `s.t[0]` is u32, the C semantics for this is wrapping subtraction. `wrapping_sub` is correct.\n\nLet me write a test to verify blake256 against a known vector:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":134,"cache_read_input_tokens":285572,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":134,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3358c92a-c7cf-4ef9-adf3-e417cf8f446f","uuid":"6a124a87-3ec7-45c5-83dd-7889adfe724b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_lrkjv4gha7cgfumu3rsdsdhxfhb2qa2xz7becrjwndfzkysmcwzq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01Q4MbjxsnRx5Be5vDeQXF2d","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-translate-lCHh9T/translated_rust/tests/blake_check.rs","content":"#![cfg(feature = \"blake\")]\n\nuse sphincsplus::blake::{blake256, blake512};\n\n#[test]\nfn blake256_empty() {\n    let mut out = [0u8; 32];\n    blake256(&mut out, b\"\", 0);\n    // BLAKE-256(\"\") = 716f6e863f744b9ac22c97ec7b76ea5f5908bc5b2f67c61510bfc4751384ea7a\n    let expected = [\n        0x71, 0x6f, 0x6e, 0x86, 0x3f, 0x74, 0x4b, 0x9a, 0xc2, 0x2c, 0x97, 0xec, 0x7b, 0x76, 0xea,\n        0x5f, 0x59, 0x08, 0xbc, 0x5b, 0x2f, 0x67, 0xc6, 0x15, 0x10, 0xbf, 0xc4, 0x75, 0x13, 0x84,\n        0xea, 0x7a,\n    ];\n    println!(\"got {:02x?}\", out);\n    assert_eq!(out, expected);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn blake256_single_byte() {\n    let mut out = [0u8; 32];\n    let input = [0u8; 1];\n    blake256(&mut out, &input, 1);\n    // BLAKE-256(0x00) per Aumasson's paper\n    println!(\"got {:02x?}\", out);\n}\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":134,"cache_read_input_tokens":285572,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":134,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3358c92a-c7cf-4ef9-adf3-e417cf8f446f","uuid":"154e2956-c0b9-45ff-8b05-1b7ad496b710"}
