{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_jwkibwfrg2wqzrv4aaucwzqo4ssprvzlvaeo7akwfbgrh7adfswa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"BLAKE-256 of empty string passes. So the BLAKE implementation matches the standard. The issue must be in how it's called. Let me look at C `blake256_mgf1`:\n\nIn `c_src/lib/blake/src/blake256.c`:\n```c\nvoid blake256_mgf1(unsigned char *out, unsigned long outlen,\n\t\t   const unsigned char *in, unsigned long inlen)\n{\n    SPX_VLA(uint8_t, inbuf, inlen+4);\n    ...\n    blake256(out, inbuf, inlen + 4);  // calls blake256 with inlen+4 bytes\n```\n\nIn my Rust:\n```rust\nblake256(out, &inbuf, (inlen + 4) as u64);  // I pass byte length too. blake256 internally does inlen*8.\n```\n\nLooking at C blake256 wrapper:\n```c\nint blake256( unsigned char *out, const unsigned char *in, unsigned long long inlen )\n{\n  blakestate256 S;  \n  blake256_init( &S );\n  blake256_update( &S, in, inlen*8 );  // takes BYTES, but passes BITS to update\n  blake256_final( &S, out );\n  return 0;\n}\n```\n\nSo `blake256(buf, in, inlen)` takes byte length. ✓\n\nBut in `blake_thash_simple.c`:\n```c\nblake256(outbuf, buf, SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES + inblocks*SPX_N);\n```\nTakes byte length too. ✓\n\nIn `hash_blake.c` `prf_addr`:\n```c\nblake256(outbuf, buf, SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES);\n```\nThis says SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES bytes — but the buffer has SK seed appended. So actually only the first SPX_N+SPX_ADDR_BYTES bytes are hashed. (This doesn't include sk_seed!) That's weird but I copy the same pattern.\n\nIn `gen_message_random` (BLAKE):\n```c\nblakeX_init(&S);\nblakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N);  // C update takes bits! So this is wrong: only SPX_N/8 bytes\n```\n\nWait — `blake256_update` and `blake512_update` in the BLAKE reference take **bits**, not bytes. So when C calls `blakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N)` with SPX_N=16, it's only updating 16 BITS = 2 bytes!\n\nThis is a bug in the C code, but since we