ze;  // 2\n    s.buf[left..left + n].copy_from_slice(&data[data_off..data_off + n]);\n    s.buflen = ((left << 3) as u64 + datalen) as i32;  // 16\n}\n```\n\nOK, after `gen_message_random` first 3 calls with bit lengths, buflen would be 16 + 16 + 8*mlen bits = small.\n\nActually wait — but the C is incorrect! The C `blakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N)` passes SPX_N **as the bit count**, but blake_update expects bits. So if SPX_N=16, this absorbs 2 bytes of sk_prf. The tail bits become part of buflen. That's the C bug we replicate.\n\nBut there's another issue — in `hash_message` for BLAKE, the C code calls `blakeX_update(&S, m, mlen)` where `mlen` is in BYTES. So this also passes byte count as bit count. So mlen bits = mlen/8 bytes get absorbed. This is a clear bug.\n\nOK, my implementation already does this. Let me check `m_off` and length passed when blake512 is involved:\n\nFor 192s blake, the digest from gen_message_random (first SPX_N bytes of the 64-byte blake512 output) becomes R.\n\nLet me compare my output vs C output for blake simple 128s on a simpler path. The KAT test difference suggests the bug is something else.\n\nLet me read the blake update again more carefully. Looking at the partial fill:\n\n```c\nif( left && ( ((datalen >> 3) & 0x3F) >= fill ) ) {\n```\n\nI have:\n```rust\nif left != 0 && (((datalen >> 3) & 0x3F) >= fill as u64) {\n```\n\nWait — `fill = 64 - left`, where `left` is < 64 (since buflen < 64*8 bits). And `(datalen >> 3) & 0x3F` is a value 0..63. So if `fill > 63` it's never >= ... wait fill = 64 - left, and left >= 1 (since `left != 0`), so fill <= 63. OK.\n\nBut there's something strange: if datalen = 1024, then (datalen >> 3) & 0x3F = 128 & 63 = 0. So even if datalen = 1024 bits, the partial fill is skipped. The next loop `while datalen >= 512` will then process two compressions, and finally store the leftover bits. That's the C code's logic.\n\nOK that's a strange bit, but the buflen=0 case (first call when left=0) doesn't trigger th