{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_3vxgjeiamesl6d4gecmpkp23vk6sc4cnbdj7m4bwvoskn5jfdcha","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"So C also returns the IV (initial state) - which is wrong but expected since `SPX_N as bits` < 64 bits = no compression and 33-bit update is within first block. Actually, with 16+16+33=65 bits, that's still less than 512 bits. So the buffer accumulates 9 bytes (72 bits → 9 bytes via `(buflen >> 3) = 8`). Then `final` flips the state into compression at the end.\n\nOK my Rust matches C for this case (both return IV). Good.\n\nSo both implementations of blake256 match. The bug must be elsewhere. Let me look at hash_blake.c prf_addr more carefully:\n\n```c\nvoid prf_addr(unsigned char *out, const spx_ctx *ctx, const uint32_t addr[8])\n{\n  unsigned char buf[2*SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES] = {0};\n  unsigned char outbuf[SPX_BLAKE256_OUTPUT_BYTES] = {0};\n\n  memcpy(buf, ctx->pub_seed, SPX_N);\n  memcpy(buf + SPX_N, addr, SPX_ADDR_BYTES);\n  memcpy(buf + SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES, ctx->sk_seed, SPX_N);\n\n  blake256(outbuf, buf, SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES);  // Note: only SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES bytes!\n\n  memcpy(out, outbuf, SPX_N);\n}\n```\n\nThe C calls `blake256` with `SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES` BYTES (not bits). Inside `blake256`:\n```c\nblake256_update( &S, in, inlen*8 );  // inlen*8 BITS\n```\nSo it absorbs `(SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES) * 8` bits = SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES bytes. ✓\n\nIn my Rust:\n```rust\nblake256(&mut outbuf, &buf, (SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES) as u64);\n```\nAnd blake256 in rust does `blake256_update(&mut s, input, inlen * 8);`. ✓\n\nOK that's correct. Then the issue is with `gen_message_random` and `hash_message`. The C calls:\n```c\nblakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N);  // BITS! So only SPX_N bits absorbed = SPX_N/8 bytes\n```\n\nMy Rust:\n```rust\nblakex_update(&mut s, sk_prf, SPX_N as u64);  // BITS\n```\nSame bug. ✓\n\nWait — but the problem is that in `gen_message_random`, the `mlen` parameter is unsigned long long bytes. C passes `mlen` as bits, so it absorbs mlen bits. My Rust does the same.\n\nBut notice: the result is 64 bytes (BLAKE-512), or 32 bytes (BLAKE-256) in `outbuf`. In C, R is SPX_N bytes. The first SPX_N bytes of the digest are copied to R. My Rust also does this. ✓\n\nHmm. Let me look more carefully at hash_message:\n\n```c\nunsigned char seed[2*SPX_N + SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES];\nblakeX_init(&S);\nblakeX_update(&S, R, SPX_N);    // BITS, but C bug\nblakeX_update(&S, pk, SPX_PK_BYTES);  // BITS, but C bug\nblakeX_update(&S, m, mlen);     // BITS, but C bug\n\nblakeX_final(&S, seed+ 2 * SPX_N);  // writes BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES bytes to seed[2*N..]\n```\n\nNote: `seed` is `2*SPX_N + SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES` bytes total, and the digest is written at offset `2*SPX_N`. That fits exactly the 32 or 64 bytes of digest. No overflow here.\n\n```c\nmemcpy(seed, R, SPX_N);  // overwrites seed[0..SPX_N]\nmemcpy(seed + SPX_N, pk, SPX_N);  // overwrites seed[SPX_N..2*SPX_N]\n\nblakeX_mgf1(bufp, SPX_DGST_BYTES, seed, 2*SPX_N + SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES);\n```\n\nmgf1 absorbs the full seed (correctly takes bytes). My Rust does the same.\n\nLet me look at my `gen_message_random` to see if I have the actual implementation correct... and `hash_message`:\n\nThe C bugs:\n1. `gen_message_random`: bits-as-bytes for sk_prf (SPX_N), optrand (SPX_N), m (mlen)\n2. `hash_message`: bits-as-bytes for R (SPX_N), pk (SPX_PK_BYTES), m (mlen)\n3. Final write of 64 bytes to a 32-byte buffer (overflow into adjacent stack)\n\nFor point 3: in `gen_message_random`, the \"R\" parameter is SPX_N bytes. But for blake-128 backends, we use blake256 which writes 32 bytes — but R is SPX_N=16 bytes. So a 16-byte stack overflow.\n\nActually wait — for 128 backends, blake256 writes 32 bytes. R is 16 bytes. So there's a 16-byte stack overflow. The C code probably \"gets away\" with this because the stack frame has space.\n\nIn my Rust I now write to a tmp 32-byte buffer first